## Power, Curfews and Wind: What to Plan For

We do not stock generators, so if your venue is a remote finca or a beach location without confirmed mains power, tell us when you book and we will size the setup to what is actually available.

A detail generic wedding checklists skip entirely: many fincas and villas on the Costa del Sol set a curfew on amplified sound, often somewhere between 11pm and 1am depending on the venue and the municipality. We do not handle the permit or negotiate the curfew with your venue, that stays between you and them, but if you tell us the curfew when you book, our live technician plans volume and timing around it rather than finding out mid reception.

Wind is the third factor. A coastal terrace or an open finca lawn brings wind a ballroom never does, and it changes where a speaker stand or lighting rig can safely stand, and how grass, sand or gravel need different cable runs than a flat indoor floor. Our technician reads the actual ground and wind exposure on site and places stands, cables and lighting accordingly rather than following a fixed indoor layout.

## Sound for the Ceremony vs the Reception, Outdoors

Open air disperses sound further than an indoor room does, since there are no walls to reflect off, so an outdoor PA is set up to project across the space rather than fill a room, the same honest point our [audio visual rental for outdoor events](/blog/audio-visual-rental-for-outdoor-events/) guide makes for any outdoor venue. A wedding adds a layer on top: ceremony and reception are usually two different zones, an aisle or altar for vows, then a dining or dance area for speeches and music. Ceremony sound is tuned for intelligibility at a seated, listening volume, while reception sound covers speeches at similar clarity, then switches to dance volume, within whatever curfew the venue sets. A live technician who stays for both zones handles that shift rather than a single drop off delivery tuned once and left alone.

Rain, heat and humidity protection for the equipment itself is a related but separate question, covered in more depth in our [guide on weather planning for outdoor rentals](/blog/weather-considerations-for-outdoor-rentals/).

